Sval: a split view shows up to four tabs side by side

Sval's split view holds two to four tabs (split-view spec §4.3).
addTabSplitView and the wrapper's addTabs already take any number of tabs;
what assumed two is the layout around them. tabpanels made one splitter and
ordered the first two panels; the wrapper reset only panels[1]'s width and
reversed only its first two tabs, and kept the list its panels are drawn
from as it was made, so a tab closing in a split of three left its panel in
the list. content-area.css ordered and spaced only columns 0 and 1.

Each panel but the last gets a splitter after it, which resizes that panel
as Firefox's one splitter resizes the first; the last panel fills the rest.
Reverse reverses every tab. The wrapper's list follows its children, and a
change in them redraws its panels. addTabSplitView, the wrapper's addTabs and
moveTabToSplitView pass a TabMetricsContext through to the TabMove each
tab raises, as tab moves elsewhere in tabbrowser do, so a split Sval rebuilds
from its tree is told from one the user makes. No chrome API reaches the
private fields involved, which is why this is a patch.

